@flexiarg snatch/lead-with-the-exchange-rule
@title Lead With the Exchange Rule
@audience policy authors
@tone practical
@style pattern
@why snatch/have-a-temperament
@see-also snatch/exchange-when-both-sides-gain

! conclusion: The trading temperament puts the exchange rule first; read the cascade the promotion produces before accepting it, because promoting a pattern with no authority takes the cascade down to that one node.

  + context: **Policy grain.** Operand: the precedence field. The instance is
    `exchange-first-overrides {:exchange-when-both-sides-gain 0}`
    (`checks/playout_snatch.clj:165`) — the harness's one re-wiring, the same
    eighteen patterns consulted in a different order.

  + IF:
    You want a temperament that leads with gains from trade rather than with the
    stop rule or the remedy.

  + HOWEVER:
    The pattern this temperament promotes carries no `@why` at all — its only
    structural directive is `@see-also`
    (`snatch/exchange-when-both-sides-gain.flexiarg:6`) — so its upward closure
    is itself. When the promotion makes it the only pattern that fires, the
    cascade the run stands on has one node and no edges. The score moves with
    it: +3 to −5 against a snatcher under G4.

  + THEN:
    Promote the exchange rule, then read the cascade that promotion produces
    before accepting the temperament. If the promoted pattern has no authority
    edge, either author one or expect a one-node cascade and say so.

  + BECAUSE:
    A temperament edits precedence; precedence decides which patterns fire;
    which patterns fire decides the up-closure; and the up-closure is the
    cascade (`checks/playout_snatch.clj:248-257`). So a precedence edit is
    silently also a membership edit one layer down, and a temperament that
    reasons only about order will not see the membership it changed.
    + evidence: `checks/snatch-cascade.edn` — under `exchange-first`, four of
      six scenarios collapse to one acting pattern and zero nodes added by
      organise; under the authored order those same scenarios add two to four.
      The single scenario where the score moves at all is g4/snatcher, +3 → −5.
